  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nA digging upper level trough will cross the region Wed morning. \nAhead of this, surface low pressure over lower MI will extend a cold \nfront to the southwest. Southerly flow ahead of this front will bring\nin more moist air, especially along a narrow, short- lived lljet \naround 50kt. The front undercuts the moist air and a sw- ne oriented \nline of showers and thunderstorms are expected to move east through \nthe region overnight. The actual front will take its time to work \nthrough the region early Wednesday, possibly with some lingering \nshowers along and east of the Scioto River through early afternoon. \n\nBy daybreak, this line should be east of the I-71 corridor. As the\nfront crosses, cooler air in central OH may hold on to lingering \nshowers through the morning, but deeper convection will have moved \neast. The transient line of storms should not pose any flooding risk.\nShear profiles weaken as the front moves from IN into OH during a\ntime of the diurnal minimum, diminishing the potential for severe\nweather from this line of storms. Models are bullish producing\nthunder for much of the overnight hours, but a well-defined line\ntells me that at least some thunder will occur throughout its \npassage.\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2) \nAfter a weak cold front crosses today, temperatures will be muted \nwith highs in the 60s. Southerly flow ahead of the next, stronger \nfront will see a slight bump Tuesday with highs in the 70s. Then, \nexpect a cooldown for the middle of the week, followed by warmer air \non Friday and moreso for the coming weekend. Temps this coming \nweekend should reach into the 80s.",
